# ── Security headers ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# frame-ancestors is here and not only in the <meta> CSP in index.html, because
# a browser *ignores* frame-ancestors when it arrives in a meta tag — it is one
# of the directives that must come over HTTP. So the app carried a rule that
# read as protection and did nothing, and anyone could put it in an iframe and
# steer a signed-in person into taps they could not see: Delete on a row, Merge
# on an account, Sign out. X-Frame-Options says the same thing to anything old
# enough not to read CSP.
#
# The meta CSP still does everything else; a second policy over HTTP is
# enforced alongside it rather than replacing it, so only the directive that
# was broken is repeated here.
#
# no-referrer is the privacy one. Without it every request for the webfonts
# below sends Google the page you were on.
/*
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()

# Read by Cloudflare Workers static assets. Only the four files that decide
# *which version of the app you get* are listed — everything with real weight
# (/assets/* and /tesseract/*) has a hashed or immutable name and keeps its
# long cache.
#
# no-cache does not mean "do not store": the browser keeps its copy and
# revalidates, so an unchanged deploy costs a 304 with an empty body. This
# exists so a CDN can never pin a stale sw.js — the one failure that makes an
# installed PWA permanently unupdatable and sends people looking for the
# clear-cache button.
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/registerSW.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/manifest.webmanifest
  Cache-Control: no-cache
